## Autonomy surface (the ask-set)

<!-- The point of a loop is to run unattended to the stop condition — NOT to
     interrupt the user for routine, safe operations. This uses Claude Code's
     NATIVE allow/ask/deny model (the de-facto industry pattern; see the ADR
     decision/2026-06-19-loop-autonomy-surface-native-permissions.md). Key fact:
     `deny` and `ask` are evaluated BEFORE the permission mode, so they hold even
     under bypassPermissions; an `allow` list CANNOT bound a permissive default.
     So DON'T enumerate an allowlist — start from "everything safe runs" and only
     resolve the borders below. -->

- **Deny (never run):** base destructive config + anything conflicting with a
  registered ADR or existing code. This-loop additions: ____
- **Pre-authorized (run without asking):** normally-prompting operations this
  loop may run unattended (pre-cleared in this interview): ____
- **Always-ask (pause every time):** irreversible / outward-facing. Defaults:
  `git push`/publish/deploy/release, dependency changes (install/update/
  downgrade), DB migrations. NOT `git commit` (local + reversible — commit
  freely). Add/remove for this loop: ____
- **Autonomous + log:** everything else that is safe, additive and non-breaking —
  including an unforeseen architectural micro-decision under your authorship.
  Proceed without stopping; the burden is to **verify the assumption (investigate,
  don't guess)** and **log the decision**. Free to investigate, read the DB, and
  take a backup without asking when it gives confidence to continue. Do NOT halt
  on a doubt that breaks nothing.

